meta { title = "Records and enums", description = "Model named data and alternatives with immutable values.", order = 8, group = "Language reference" }

Use type for a named record with fields, and enum for a value that is one of several declared alternatives. Declarations are nominal: equal field layouts in unrelated declarations do not make them the same type.

h2 { "Construct and update a record" }

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/io" import println, IoError
from "std/fmt" import format

type Point:
  x: i64
  y: i64

fn main() -> unit ! IoError:
  original = Point { x: 2, y: 3 }
  moved = original with { x: 12 }
  output: i64 = original.x + moved.x + moved.y

  try println(format("{}", output))
  return ()
""" }

This displays 17: original.x is 2, moved.x is 12 and moved.y is 3. Field initializer order follows source order. A with expression produces a new record; original remains unchanged. All required fields must be supplied once with compatible types. Records can contain supported Strings, collections, nested records or enums, and function values; the contained values are kept alive automatically. Copying a record preserves the backing aliases of its array fields. Use copy on an array field when the new record needs independent element storage.

h2 { "Represent alternatives" }

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/io" import println, IoError
from "std/fmt" import format

enum Reading:
  Missing
  Value:
    measurement: i64

fn readOrDefault(reading: Reading) -> i64:
  return match reading:
    Reading.Missing: 0
    Reading.Value { measurement }: measurement

fn main() -> unit ! IoError:
  output: i64 = readOrDefault(Reading.Value { measurement: 42 })

  try println(format("{}", output))
  return ()
""" }

This displays 42. Only the selected alternative exists as a language value. Read its payload by matching that variant. A final _ branch may handle remaining alternatives; missing, repeated and unreachable cases are diagnosed. Enum values do not gain record-style updates or implicit numeric ordinals.

h2 { "Cross calls and workers" }

Records and enums keep their contained values alive automatically and can be passed through supported CPU worker tasks. Importing a declaration under a different name does not create a new type. GPU helpers can construct and pass local nonrecursive records/enums containing numeric/bool scalars, numeric/bool arrays and nested records. GPU task entry arguments and results cannot be records or enums; keep those values inside the placed helper.

